<Base
  title="Lagom — Filipe Fantini"
  description="Lagom is a personal laboratory: one small server, a public earthquake feed, and a record of what was decided, measured, and left open."
  canonical="https://ofantini.com/projects/lagom"
  current="projects"
  inventory="none"
>
  <section class="essay">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Laboratory</p>
      <h1>Lagom, end to end.</h1>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Lagom is not a system I run for an employer. It is the place where a data platform is
          built small on purpose, written down, and measured. The Swedish word means not too
          little, not too much.
        </p>
        <p>
          A public feed comes in through a job that starts and exits. The rows land, get cleaned
          and modelled, and the schedule keeps a history of the run. What a visitor should
          eventually see is static files, not a connection to the machine. That gallery is not
          built yet. The pages under this one say what is running, what was decided, and what is
          still only a proposal.
        </p>
        <p>
          One tension is left in the open, on purpose. The scheduled job loads a lake. The accepted
          storage decision still says every layer lives in Postgres until a spike passes. These
          pages do not close that.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Series here="/projects/lagom" />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
